{
  "_note": "ENV-01: a test level, not a level to ship. One plain lit room 16 x 16 m, a player start, an exit pad in the room, and an empty arenas list so no lock ever shuts anything (the page's exit works from the first frame). The column is a shell box, 0.8 x 3 x 0.8 m at [-3, 1.5, 0], indestructible. col-coat is E01: one piece of four volumes, 0.3 m thick on all four sides, tied to the floor so its lowest voxel row is the bedrock anchor (build.ts fillPieces) and the coat cannot fall off as an island. Stage 2 adds E02: two toy crate stacks. A crate is 0.6 m: a hollow box of six 0.1 m cardboard panels (review: a solid crate could not be shot through in play) with twelve foam-ink (dark) bars on its edges, each bar 0.2 m thick so it overlaps the body by 0.1 m and stands 0.1 m proud of the panel it frames (voxels are 0.1 m, so one voxel proud). Each stack is ONE floor anchored piece, so fillPieces makes only its lowest row bedrock, a 0.6 x 0.1 x 0.6 wood plinth hidden under the bottom crate: the crates above hang from that row through their own voxels, so carving a bottom crate out leaves the top crate with no path to bedrock and it falls as one crate-shaped island. That is why a stack is one piece and not three: a crate of its own would either be floor anchored (its own bottom row becomes bedrock, so it can never fall) or have no anchor at all. stack-a puts the top crate half a crate (0.3 m) over the left bottom crate only, clear of the right one, so it cannot stay up on the right crate when the left one goes; stack-b is a column of two, the plainest version of the same topple. Two crates plus the plinth is 1.3 m, under the player's 1.5 m mantle (KIT 5). Metres, Y up, -z is north, positions are box centres, sizes are full extents.",
